---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import FaqBlock from '../../components/FaqBlock.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import { SITE_URL } from '../../site';

const rows = [
  { dimension: 'Transit time', direct: 'Shorter — no hub wait', transshipment: 'Longer — adds a hub connection' },
  { dimension: 'Cost', direct: 'Often higher (premium schedule)', transshipment: 'Often lower (hub consolidation)' },
  { dimension: 'Reliability', direct: 'Fewer hand-offs, fewer failure points', transshipment: 'Two sailings to miss, not one' },
  { dimension: 'Schedule frequency', direct: 'Thinner on niche lanes', transshipment: 'More frequent on small lanes' },
  { dimension: 'Typical on', direct: 'Rotterdam, Hamburg, Antwerp, Felixstowe', transshipment: 'Valencia, Genoa via Med hubs' },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is direct shipping always faster than transshipment?',
    a: 'Almost always, but the gap varies. Direct saves the hub connection and the waiting time at the transshipment port. On a congested hub, transshipment can add a week or more — though a well-scheduled connection can be nearly as fast as direct.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is transshipment cheaper than direct?',
    a: 'Usually, because hubs consolidate cargo and let carriers fill larger vessels. But the saving is not guaranteed once you factor in the risk of a missed connection and the extra handling. Price the total, not the ocean line item.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which European ports are served directly from China?',
    a: 'The Northwest European range — Rotterdam, Hamburg, Antwerp and Felixstowe — has the densest direct China calls. Mediterranean ports like Valencia and Genoa are more often served by transshipment via hubs such as Tanger Med or Malta.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the risk of transshipment?',
    a: 'The main risk is a missed connection: your first sailing is delayed, the feeder leaves without your container, and you wait for the next. It is a second schedule to depend on, which is why direct is valued for time-sensitive cargo.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    type: 'FAQPage',
    data: {
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: f.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
      })),
    },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Direct vs Transshipment Shipping: China to Europe"
  description="Direct versus transshipped ocean freight from China to Europe: transit time, cost, reliability, and which European ports each serves."
  canonical={`${SITE_URL}/europe/direct-vs-transshipment/`}
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Europe', href: '/europe/' },
          { label: 'Direct vs Transshipment' },
        ]}
      />
      <h1>Direct vs transshipment: China to Europe</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The word "direct" sells, but it is not always the right buy — the
        choice is a trade between time and a second schedule to depend on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <blockquote class="tldr">
      <p>
        <strong>TL;DR:</strong> Direct sailings are faster and more reliable but
        often dearer and only available on the dense Northwest European lanes.
        Transshipment is usually cheaper and keeps small lanes viable, but it
        adds a hub connection and a second schedule you can miss.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Side by side</h2>
    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'dimension', label: 'Dimension' },
        { key: 'direct', label: 'Direct' },
        { key: 'transshipment', label: 'Transshipment' },
      ]}
      rows={rows}
      caption="Indicative comparison — confirm the routing on your specific lane."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Europe splits by geography</h2>
    <p>
      The direct-versus-transshipment choice is not uniform across Europe — it
      splits by geography. The Northwest European range — Rotterdam, Hamburg,
      Antwerp and Felixstowe — carries the densest direct China calls, because
      the volumes justify full vessels. Mediterranean ports — Valencia, Genoa
      and to some extent Le Havre — are more often fed by transshipment: a
      China sailing discharges at a hub like Tanger Med, Malta or Piraeus, and
      a feeder carries the container the final leg.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why a Mediterranean port can look slower on paper — it often
      carries an extra hub stop — yet still be the cheapest total route for
      Iberian or Italian cargo. The transshipment is the price of reaching a
      thinner lane, not a sign of a worse route.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>The risk nobody prices</h2>
    <p>
      Transshipment's real cost is not the extra sailing — it is the missed
      connection. A direct container depends on one schedule; a transshipped
      container depends on two. If the first leg is delayed and the feeder
      leaves without your box, you wait for the next feeder, and a "cheaper"
      route quietly becomes a slower one. For time-sensitive cargo, direct
      buys you insurance against that second schedule. For patient, price-driven
      cargo, transshipment's saving is usually worth the small extra risk.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <FaqBlock items={faqs} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <Cta />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
